+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers can correct the same visit independently after comparing notes. A correction may leave a tablet while offline and arrive days after the state it was edited from was fetched. The record store must decide what a visit's current state is when those submissions overlap, without treating arrival order as evidence of authority and without silently discarding a submitted edit. The policy determines the submission envelope, the shape of coordinator review work, and which visits can enter the Friday CSV, so it is settled before tablet-sync and review-export are designed.
+
+Both credible policies preserve every original submission. One keeps each submitted visit whole; the other combines changes at field level. Last-write-wins is outside the domain because it discards a submitted edit.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Preserve whole-visit scientific intent — major.** A submitted visit expresses relationships among its fields: a species identification and its cover estimate, an observer list and the observation date. The policy must not create a current visit by combining fields from different submissions in a way no worker submitted.
+- **Coordinator review volume — major.** The team has two coordinators and a weekly Friday export. Every visit held for review is work they must finish before that export, so the number of visits that cannot become current automatically carries operational weight.
+- **Maintained merge-rule burden — major.** Any rule set that decides how field values combine must be maintained as fields, species-entry structure, and team practice change. Rules the team cannot maintain become a source of silent miscombination.
+- **Explainability of the current value — moderate.** A coordinator or the science partner may need to know which submission produced an exported value and why competing submissions did not.
+- **Tablet submission and storage cost — minor.** Tablets may be offline for 48 hours and submit over unreliable connections. Whole payloads are small, but repeated transmission still costs bandwidth and retry time.
+- **Operational fit for two coordinators — moderate.** The workflow must be operable without a dedicated systems administrator, per the project tenet.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each accepted edit is a complete visit revision that names the revision it was edited from. The store keeps every revision as an immutable whole. Revisions that share a base but are not each other's ancestors become competing heads; a visit with more than one head is unresolved. A coordinator compares the competing whole revisions and selects one, which becomes the sole current revision. No field is taken from a different submission than the one selected.
+
+**Demonstration.** Workers A and B both edit revision `r1`. A submits a complete visit based on `r1`, producing `r2`. B submits a complete visit also based on `r1`, producing `r3`. The visit has heads `{r2, r3}` and is unresolved. A coordinator selects `r2`; `r2` becomes current, `r3` is retained as superseded, and the resolution records both `r2` and `r3` as contributors.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Preserve whole-visit scientific intent: strong.** The current value is always exactly one worker's submitted visit, so no scientific relationship is combined across submissions.
+- **Coordinator review volume: weak.** Two edits that touch unrelated fields still produce competing whole revisions and require coordinator selection.
+- **Maintained merge-rule burden: strong.** The store compares revision identity and head sets; it maintains no field-level combination rules.
+- **Explainability of the current value: strong.** The export points to one revision with one author, a named base, and a recorded selection decision.
+- **Tablet submission and storage cost: weak.** Every submission and retry carries the complete visit payload, and the tablet retains the whole draft.
+- **Operational fit for two coordinators: strong.** Review is a comparison and a single selection; there are no rules to tune or interpret.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger review queue for whole-visit fidelity and near-zero merge maintenance.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each edit supplies its base state and a structured change set. The server compares the base, the current state, and the change. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically; changes to the same field are held for review with field-level provenance. Rules define how each field type combines: scalar fields such as observation date and notes, observer lists with additions, removals, and renames, and species entries keyed by species identity. A combined visit can become current without a coordinator if no field overlaps.
+
+**Demonstration.** Workers A and B both start from `r1`. A corrects an observer name; B adds a species cover entry. The server combines both into the current visit automatically because the changed fields do not overlap. If A and B both change the cover estimate for the same species, that field is held for review while unrelated fields may already be current.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Preserve whole-visit scientific intent: weak.** Automatic combination creates a visit no worker submitted. The rules also cannot infer that a species identification and its cover estimate are scientifically related, so a change to one and a change to the other can be combined as if they were independent.
+- **Coordinator review volume: strong.** Disjoint edits, which are common when workers correct different parts of a visit, reach the export without coordinator involvement.
+- **Maintained merge-rule burden: weak.** The team must define and maintain rules for every field type and species-entry behavior, and revisit them as the survey form changes.
+- **Explainability of the current value: partial.** Field-level provenance can show which submission supplied each field, but the combined value is explained by rules rather than by a single submitted visit.
+- **Tablet submission and storage cost: partial.** Change sets are smaller to transmit and retry than whole payloads, but tablets must retain base snapshots and enough structure to produce changes.
+- **Operational fit for two coordinators: partial.** Less review volume helps, but the coordinators depend on merge rules they did not author and cannot easily verify for scientific relationships.
+
+**Summary.** Trades merge-rule maintenance and cross-submission combination for a smaller review queue and smaller submissions.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions is selected. It wins the high-importance criteria on whole-visit scientific intent, maintained merge-rule burden, and explainability; field-aware merge wins review volume, which is the cost the team accepts. The review queue is expected to remain operable because independent corrections to the same visit are occasional rather than the normal case, and the two coordinators already review submissions before the Friday export. If unresolved volume grows to the point that too many visits miss the export, that is the signal to revisit this decision, not a reason to combine fields silently.
+
+The decision turns on the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*: both options preserve originals, but whole-visit revisions also preserves each submission as the unit that becomes current. It also serves *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* by removing a rule set the team would have to maintain and trust. It is taken against the review-volume advantage of field-aware merging.
+
+Committing to this policy forecloses automatic combination of unrelated field edits for the first release. A visit with any competing heads waits for a coordinator even when the edits are disjoint. It does not foreclose later reconsideration: original whole payloads and their base links are retained, so a future field-aware policy could be evaluated against the same history.
+
+## Downstream Obligations
+
+### Tablet-sync
+
+- Retain the revision ID from the last retrieval as the draft's base revision, and send the complete visit payload with the submission envelope.
+- Do not choose a winner locally and do not rebase a stale draft. A stale base is accepted and may create a competing head.
+- Reuse the same idempotency key when retrying an unacknowledged submission.
+- Display resolved versus unresolved status. For an unresolved visit, show the competing head revisions, including the worker's own, rather than presenting one as current.
+- Refresh the base revision and head set from the server before submitting, and accommodate an acceptance response that reports head revision IDs.
+- Carry the larger whole-visit payload through offline retry without assuming the earlier payload is still current.
+
+### Review-export
+
+- Build the review queue from visits with more than one head, and compare competing whole revisions with author and receipt time.
+- Submit resolutions with the selected revision and the full reviewed head set; handle a conflict by refreshing the head set and reviewing again.
+- Exclude unresolved visits from the CSV; export the current revision of resolved visits only.
+- Record the coordinator's identity and note with the resolution outcome.
+- Present no field-level merge view and maintain no merge rules; comparison is revision-level.
+- Handle a resolved visit reopening when a later submission names a superseded revision as its base.
