-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Retrieval
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Visit retrieval returns the visit ID, plot ID, status, and revision data:
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+- When resolved, it returns the current revision ID and that revision's payload.
+- When unresolved, it returns every head revision ID with each head's payload, author, base revision, and receipt time.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Revision-history retrieval returns all revisions in server receipt order together with any resolutions. This history is for inspection; it does not change which revision is current.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+The unresolved-work queue is a derived list of visits whose status is unresolved. Each entry carries the visit ID, plot ID, and head revision summaries. No separate pending-work state is stored.
+
+A visit is exportable only when resolved. An export uses the current revision's payload and excludes unresolved visits.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A resolution request contains:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `visitId` | Visit being resolved |
+| `selectedRevisionId` | Head revision that becomes current |
+| `reviewedHeadRevisionIds` | Head revisions the coordinator compared |
+| `note` | Optional coordinator explanation |
+
+The resolution is accepted only when all of the following hold:
+
+- The caller is authenticated as a coordinator.
+- The visit is unresolved.
+- `selectedRevisionId` is one of the visit's current heads.
+- The set of `reviewedHeadRevisionIds` equals the visit's current head set.
+
+A mismatch means a new head arrived after the coordinator retrieved the visit; the request is rejected as a conflict so the coordinator can retrieve the updated head set and review it. This prevents a resolution that did not account for a competing submission already on the server.
+
+On acceptance the store records the coordinator ID, selected revision, contributing revision IDs (the entire reviewed head set, including the selected revision), and decision time. The head set becomes the selected revision alone. Superseded revisions and the resolution record are retained.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+| Consumer | Contract used | Obligation |
+|---|---|---|
+| tablet-sync | Submission envelope, acceptance response, visit retrieval | Retain the revision ID from the last retrieval as the draft's base; refresh the base from retrieval before submitting; display resolved versus unresolved status rather than choosing a winner locally. |
+| review-export | Visit retrieval, unresolved-work queue, resolution request | Present unresolved heads for comparison, exclude unresolved visits from the CSV, and handle resolution conflicts by refreshing the head set. |
+
+Neither consumer defines its own concurrency semantics; both link to this node's contract.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent edit policy | [Whole-visit revisions](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md) | Field-aware three-way merge; last-write-wins | Preserves each submitted visit as a unit and avoids maintained merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing; a larger review queue is accepted in exchange. |
+| Resolution semantics | Select one submitted revision | Merge selected fields; coordinator authors a new revision | Keeps the whole-visit intent and avoids a combination no worker submitted. |
+| Stale-base submissions | Accept as competing revisions | Reject a stale base; rebase automatically to the current head | Rejecting discards a submitted edit; rebasing silently changes the state the worker edited. |
+| Export eligibility | Exactly one head | Export the latest received revision; export with conflict markers | A visit with competing heads has no single defensible current value. |
+| Resolution race control | Reviewed head set must equal the current head set | Let the last resolution win | Prevents a coordinator from resolving without seeing a head that arrived after retrieval. |
+| Idempotency scope | Per-author key with identical-payload check | Global key; no idempotency key | Makes retries safe without duplicating revisions or colliding across workers. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions, with competing revisions resolved by coordinator selection.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether a coordinator may author a corrected whole revision when no submitted head is acceptable, rather than selecting one head.
+2. Whether a coordinator may reopen a resolved visit without a new worker submission.
+3. Revision-retention policy after export, including whether old revisions and resolutions are archived or deleted.
+4. How coordinators are notified that a new head arrived while a comparison is open.
+5. Identity-provider integration and role provisioning for workers and coordinators.
+6. History pagination for visits with many revisions.
